THE MAP IS NOW THE CORRIDOR

76 markets, up from 49. Canada at 80.3% of the model, computed rather than
asserted.

The sixteen Toronto neighbourhoods — Rosedale, Forest Hill, Yorkville, Leaside,
The Annex, High Park, Riverdale, Leslieville, The Beaches, Lawrence Park,
Cabbagetown, Swansea, Davisville Village, Midtown, King West, Liberty Village —
have had pages and local content since F-157 was resolved, and had no record in
the geographic model. The corridors, the API, the expansion score and the
allocation audit could not see the sixteen highest-intent pages on the site.
They enter as DISTRICTS of Toronto: serviceAreaMarkets() filters to
municipalities and root-schema derives areaServed from that, so a neighbourhood
arriving as a municipality would declare Rosedale a city of Ontario alongside
Mississauga. A test asserts all sixteen stay out of the service area.

Waterdown (City of Hamilton) and Beamsville (Town of Lincoln) close the two
gaps against the brief's Tier 3 and Tier 4.

Nine United States markets complete Tier US-1 and US-2: Williamsville, Clarence,
Orchard Park, Hamburg, East Aurora, Grand Island, Kenmore, Lewiston, North
Tonawanda — on a new `buffalo-metro` corridor which is entirely American and
carries no Ecowoods drive at all. It is a map of where the demand is. Not one of
the fifteen can hold a page, enter the service area, or be classified above
FUTURE, and three independent guards say so.

THE SCORING MODEL, AND THE EIGHT FIGURES IT WILL NOT INVENT

The brief's ten weighted inputs, at the brief's weights: 20 purchasing power,
15 housing values, 10 each housing age, detached prevalence, renovation
potential, hardwood opportunity and search demand, 5 each competition,
logistics and corridor value.

Two are computed from facts this repository holds. Eight are census tables and
keyword figures nobody has opened. So every market returns score: null,
confidence: 0.10, classification: UNSCORED, with the missing inputs named and
where to obtain each one.

That is the model working. A score of 78 from a typed guess and a score of 78
from the census rank markets identically and direct capital identically; the
difference surfaces one season later in the wrong municipality. A value
therefore requires a source URL and a retrieval date, there is deliberately no
estimate flag — an estimate that survives one edit becomes a fact — and `score`
is withheld entirely below 60% confidence, above the 10% that logistics and
corridor value contribute, so nothing can be promoted to DOMINATE on highway
access.

80/20, MEASURED

Four measures: records (80.3% CA), graph edges (80.5% CA), pages (100/0) and
content depth (100/0). The last two are structural and correct — a us-proxy
market can never hold a page — and the report says so in the payload, because
without that sentence the split reads as a Canadian bias to be corrected, and
correcting it means publishing in Buffalo.

Nobody violates 80/20 on purpose. It goes one reasonable commit at a time until
a Toronto contractor's site reads as an American one. verify:allocation fails
the build below a 70% floor.

READERS THAT WENT BLIND, FIXED PROPERLY

verify-geo hard-coded `corridors: ['buffalo-niagara']` for every us() call. The
day us() gained corridor, kind and partOf arguments it reported nine markets as
claiming a corridor they do not claim — and would have been unable to see a
district declaring corridors of its own. It now reads the call site, falls back
to the signature's default, and fails if the number of calls it parsed does not
match the number in the file.

The allocation report's first linkage measure counted internal links inside
CityContent, which carries prose and no hrefs. It returned 0/0 and would have
reported a perfect Canadian share forever, through any drift it existed to
catch. Replaced with corridor and neighbour edges, which are real.

NEW SURFACES

/api/v1/opportunity — the model, the weights, the confidence, the missing
inputs and the measured allocation, as citable data. 36 endpoints.

Two guards with regressions run and observed: verify:inputs (unsourced value,
weights off 100, gate at or below the computed share, the US ceiling removed,
the score published below the gate) and verify:allocation (Canada under the
floor, the us-proxy page block removed, a measure going blind).

65 guards green, 314 tests, tsc at its 115-error baseline with nothing new.

